<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andhra Pradesh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andhra Pradesh is a state on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, inhabited mainly by Telugu speakers, with its capital at Amaravati. It was created in 1953 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-state">Andhra State</a>, the first Indian state formed on linguistic lines, and enlarged in 1956 with the Telugu districts of Hyderabad State. In 2014 its north-western part became the separate state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>. Its history includes the Satavahana, Eastern Chalukya and Kakatiya dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh.jpg" alt="Andhra Pradesh" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godavari_satellite_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1950 Hyderabad was a state of the Indian Union with Osman Ali Khan as its Rajpramukh, until the reorganisation of 1956 divided it on linguistic lines among <b>Andhra Pradesh</b>, Bombay State and Mysore State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Vijayanagara architecture, with its tall gateway towers, pillared halls carved with horses and mythical beasts and large temple-city complexes, set a style followed across the south, and many temples in Tamil Nadu and <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> were rebuilt or enlarged under its rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It once reached further: Tamil or its dialects were the major language of administration, literature and daily life in Kerala until the 12th century CE, and Tamil inscriptions were made in southern <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> until then and in southern Karnataka from the 10th to the 14th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the southernmost part of the Indian subcontinent: present-day Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry, Lakshadweep and southern parts of <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> and Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It became part of Andhra State in 1953 and now lies in <b>Andhra Pradesh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banganapalle-state">Banganapalle State</a></li><li><blockquote>Their main capital was Hemavati, in <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> near the Karnataka border.</blockquote><a href="/state/nolamba-dynasty">Nolamba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
